WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.080
What's a quasiperfect number? Well, a

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.840
perfect number is a number that's equal

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.080
to the sum of its proper divisors, like

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.920
six. Its divisors are 1, 2, 3

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.080
and 6, but that's not a proper divisor.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.440
1 plus 2 plus 3

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.480
equals 6, so 6 is a perfect number.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.800
A quasiperfect number? Well that's a

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.720
number where the sum of the proper
divisors

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.160
is one more than the number itself,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.080
that's a quasiperfect number! Only thing

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.160
is nobody's ever found any.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.679
But it's still an open question as to

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:31.279
whether or not there are

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:33.440
any; and so as a high school student I

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.800
actually worked on that problem and

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:35.760
proved that

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.840
if there were any quasiperfect numbers

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.680
lurking out there they had to at least

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.607
satisfy certain properties.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:42.960
(Brady: Of all the things, like)

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.480
(there's all these glamorous things like)

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.480
(prime numbers and 
proper perfect numbers,)

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.360
(why did you pick)

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.480
(this obscure little one that has this- )
- Ha!

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.079
Well I was a student at a

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.760
National Science Foundation summer

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.440
program in mathematics that-

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.039
that was held at Hampshire

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:01.280
college in Massachusetts.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.359
And it was a wonderful six week program

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.600
and learned lots of math;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.760
and amongst other things, one afternoon, I

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.360
went to the Hampshire college library,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.880
which didn't have that all many books on

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.000
mathematics but did have

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.400
some book on number theory. And I pulled

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.439
out this book

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:17.520
and I found that - I even remember - there

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.880
was there was a description of

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.400
quasiperfect numbers and

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.560
some result that had been proved about

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.439
them by someone called Sierpiński.

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:27.439
And we'd been talking about perfect

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:28.720
numbers and other things and I was

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.640
struck by that result and I wondered,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.799
could I do any better? And it turned out

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:34.400
I could do somewhat better!

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.799
And I ended up writing a Westinghouse

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:38.799
prize contest paper about that; the

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:40.000
Westinghouse is now the-

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.159
the Intel prize and it ended up winning

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.090
the thing.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.799
But mostly what was cool to me was I

00:01:46.799 --> 00:01:49.520
could see something in a textbook

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.680
and find that I could extend, even by a

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.720
little bit, about

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.799
what's kind of a relatively unimportant

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:56.079
subject

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.000
all of human knowledge by some tiny

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.680
little bit. And once you learn that

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.830
that's kind of addictive.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.080
Oh I confess I don't even remember all

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.040
the details about what it was, it's now
been

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.480
uh- let's see, you know, 
more than 35 years

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.200
since I proved that; but it was a set of

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.480
constraints on

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.959
what the structure of the number was, uh

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:24.319
I think it had to be

00:02:24.319 --> 00:02:26.160
one more than a square and some other

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.920
things and how many prime factors -

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.440
and the truth is I haven't followed

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.519
quasiperfect numbers since then.

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:33.360
It's probably not a really really

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.200
important question per se,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.560
although the question about the

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.400
distribution of divisors and their sums

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.480
that's a good and important question in

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.200
in number theory. But it was more that it

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.239
was a totally understandable, tractable

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:46.959
question

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:48.239
that a high school student could

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:50.000
understand, that wasn't hard to pose

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.319
the definition of it; and to realize that

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:53.840
you could pose a definition in a

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:54.879
sentence

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.560
and that all the mathematicians in the

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.720
world still didn't know if there were
any,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.319
meant that there were just an infinite

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:02.400
number of really cool mysteries out

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.120
there;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.319
they were accessible in some way. So,

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:08.239
you know, I loved mathematics for that. As

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:09.519
a high school student I loved

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:11.120
mathematics, I did my

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.200
undergraduate degree in pure mathematics

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.120
at Princeton and my PhD

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.800
in pure mathematics at Oxford; and

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.480
you know just loved it, I think so many

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.120
people love mathematics because

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.319
within your mind there is a world that

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:28.640
that you can create and experiment in

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.400
and find the regularities in and the

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.440
laws of - without needing a laboratory,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.080
it's all accessible.
- (Brady: It doesn't seem the)

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.680
(default position for a high school)

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.360
(student to read about that)

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.000
(and then think: let's nut out a proof.)

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.360
(They're more likely to read that and)

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.959
(think, oh I wonder if I can find one? To)

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.720
(start taking stabs in the dark. But you)

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.920
(went and made like a)

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.400
(a proper structured mathematical proof?)

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.680
Well, you know, I think

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.239
I think the cool thing- this particular

00:03:56.239 --> 00:03:58.000
National Science Foundation program

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.680
that that I went to as high school

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.720
student had as its motto

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.840
'Find Patterns Make Conjectures Prove

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.879
Things'. I think that sums up what

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:10.720
mathematics is about.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.879
And we gave emphasis to all of those; so

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:14.319
I went there as a student then I went

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:16.000
back and taught for three summers as a

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.160
faculty member at it while I was in
college,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.000
teaching high school students about it.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.440
And I think all three have an equally

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.120
important role in mathematics; finding

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.800
pattern,

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.000
people- non-mathematicians get the sense

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.199
that mathematics is this

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:31.120
really dry and boring thing, somebody

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.560
gives you a problem and asks you to

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.080
prove it or calculate a number or

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.400
something. Of course what mathematicians

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.120
really do

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.280
is they look for the regularities in the

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.320
world and the discoveries that get made

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.600
are the most interesting part of

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.479
mathematics. Then when you see a pattern

00:04:46.479 --> 00:04:47.919
you don't quite know how to describe it

00:04:47.919 --> 00:04:49.919
and phrase it and the act of phrasing a

00:04:49.919 --> 00:04:51.040
conjecture:

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.400
I believe that this pattern I- I'm seeing
means

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:57.120
that there's an infinite number of twin

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.960
primes, for example.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.000
Or that there are no solutions- that,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.600
that all the solutions to a certain type

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.080
of equation; which primes can be

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.919
written as the sum of two squares, have a

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:09.199
certain form.

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:10.960
Learning how to take a pattern and turn

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.639
it into a rigorous conjecture,

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:13.840
that's also an important work of

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.600
mathematicians. And then finally when you

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.360
have a rigorous conjecture

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.280
figuring out how you could possibly

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.560
prove it. And so

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.120
I think we learned most of all that that

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.479
was what mathematics was about and

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:25.440
therefore

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.280
when you saw something, and there was a

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.039
conjecture about it, you wanted to think

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:30.479
about how you might prove that.

00:05:30.479 --> 00:05:32.000
So yeah it was actually perfectly

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.560
natural for, you know, math kids. I was a

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.800
math kid in New York City,

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.320
I was a lower middle class kid from

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.720
Brooklyn. I had the opportunity to go to

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.639
Stuyvesant high school in Manhattan, one

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:45.759
of the- one of New York
City's absolutely free

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:47.759
public but exam schools

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:51.440
for math and science. And

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.919
well I- I loved it. I got to Stuyvesant

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:55.759
high school in ninth grade, discovered

00:05:55.759 --> 00:05:57.520
there was this math team, people meeting

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.800
at eight o'clock in the morning

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.319
at Stuyvesant high school, coming in an

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:02.639
hour before classes to get together to

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:05.759
do problems. And so the math team quickly

00:06:05.759 --> 00:06:07.840
became a home for me, I came in an hour

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.759
early, went to the math team every day

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:11.759
for the course of four years, eventually

00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:13.520
became the captain of that math team, and

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.280
in those those ancient

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.280
days before computers and the internet

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.360
or everything there was a special

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.639
trove of problems that

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:24.560
we had to work on and they were all

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.440
stored in 'The Shopping Bag'.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.360
The Stuyvesant high school math team had

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:30.960
'The Shopping Bag'

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.360
filled with problems and it was in the

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.360
care of the captain of the math team

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.199
that the shopping bag was kept.

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:40.479
(Brady: Because you won
this really prestigious prize)

00:06:40.479 --> 00:06:42.800
(because of these numbers, I'm imagining)

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.800
(you feel some kind of-)

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.880
(do you still find any kind of attachment)

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.319
(to it? And like would you love to find)

00:06:48.319 --> 00:06:49.360
(out if there is one or have you just)

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:50.479
(moved on now and it's like- ?)

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.160
Well, I mean it would be cool to find out

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.919
but I've- I've moved on since. There are

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:56.550
other things I'm fascinated by.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.250
[Music]

00:07:06.479 --> 00:07:08.240
I think what was- what was wonderful

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:10.080
about the

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.680
summer high school program, was wonderful

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.360
about the Westinghouse contest,

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.720
what was wonderful about the Stuyvesant

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.639
high school math team

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:19.199
was much more the community of people

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:20.000
who cared.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.400
What it really meant to me was there was

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.639
a life in this, you could actually make a
living,

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:26.160
you could actually have a community, you

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.160
could actually have friends and colleagues

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.160
who cared about deeply intellectual

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.319
problems. And you know growing up where I

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:35.759
did in the outskirts of Brooklyn, not

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:37.199
everybody

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:40.479
was like that. And so for a student to

00:07:40.479 --> 00:07:42.639
discover that there's a world of people

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:44.080
who care about such things

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:46.319
and live for such things; whether it's

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:48.080
mathematics or biology

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:50.400
or mathematics and biology - things I do

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.039
today - that was the real 
eye-opening thing; and

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:55.120
if quasiperfect numbers was a way to

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.800
get there that was great

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.000
but I don't necessarily have an

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.680
attachment to them but they were a

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.120
wonderful guide along the

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:02.800
along the trail. 
- (You don't feel like you)

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.779
(owe them one?)

00:08:03.779 --> 00:08:07.110
No, no I think, I think we're 
square.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.720
(Do you think there's one out there?)

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.750
(What's your- what's your gut tell you?)

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.160
Oh, goodness

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.540
um; well-

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.479
It's really tough, I don't have a good

00:08:20.479 --> 00:08:21.919
gut feeling on it. I mean,

00:08:21.919 --> 00:08:23.199
you know, I'd like to say there aren't

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:25.120
any, because none have been found,

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.080
but I don't think there's a tremendously

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.929
good reason to believe there aren't any.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.240
[Preview] The iPhone - which is
gorgeous, I love my

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.719
iPhone - seems so important
and directly valuable;

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:44.480
but all the fundamental physics and the

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.240
electronics, the computer science that

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.200
makes an iPhone

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.040
possible - they're crucial to the

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:50.640
existence of an iphone.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.399
And Steve Jobs would have said that, and

00:08:52.399 --> 00:08:54.640
Tim Cook at Apple will say that;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.600
that the beautiful things you encounter

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:58.800
in your daily life,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.680
whether it's an iPhone or a drug that'll

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.040
cure a disease...

